<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uija of Baekje</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/660">660</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uija was the last king of Baekje, reigning from 641 to 660. He won early victories over Silla, taking some forty fortresses in 642, but in 660 an alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and Silla invaded, defeated his last army at Hwangsanbeol and captured the capital Sabi. He surrendered and was taken to the Tang capital, where he died the same year.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a>, Uija was the eldest son of King Mu; his mother was Lady Sataek, daughter of the minister Sataek Jeokdeok, who became Empress Dowager Munjeong in his reign. A legend in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a> that Mu was a peasant who married Princess Seonhwa of Silla is not considered orthodox history. Uija was made crown prince in January 632. In his time Goguryeo, under Yŏn Kaesomun, took an aggressive stance against Silla and the Tang, and Silla answered by allying closely with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, which left Baekje threatened from both sides.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Though friendly with the Tang at first, Uija soon joined Goguryeo against Silla. In 642 he sent a force of 10,000 to take Silla's Daeya Fortress and kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muyeol-of-silla">Kim Chunchu</a>'s daughter and son-in-law, and in the following year Baekje and Goguryeo attacked Silla again to cut its diplomatic route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. When Silla and the Tang attacked Goguryeo in 645 he took seven more castles, and in 655 the two kingdoms struck Silla's northern border. Early in his reign he tried to curb the power of the aristocracy, but the court was weakened by rivalry among the nobles, corruption and decadence.</p><p>Frustrated by Yŏn Kaesomun, the Silla-Tang alliance decided to strike Goguryeo's ally first. In 660 the Tang navy defeated Baekje's fleet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-yu-sin">Kim Yu-sin</a>'s Silla army defeated Gye Baek. As Sabi, in present-day Buyeo, was surrounded, Uija and the crown prince escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongju">Ungjin</a>, in present-day Gongju, but surrendered when the capital fell.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>He was taken to the Tang with his sons Buyeo Hyo and Buyeo Yung, 88 retainers and 12,807 Baekje peasants. Another son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyeo-pung">Buyeo Pung</a>, who had been sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> as a hostage in 643, returned and was declared king by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boksin">Gwisil Boksin</a> in an attempt to restore Baekje, but was captured and exiled. The Japanese Nihon Shoki alone names his queen, Eun'go, and blames her for the kingdom's fall. Uija was his personal name, as he received no posthumous name. In 2000 his remains were brought back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and buried in a new tomb at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neungsan-ri">Neungsan-ri</a> in Buyeo County, near Sabi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uija of Baekje” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Baekje regained the initiative under King <b>Uija</b>, who took some forty Silla fortresses and the stronghold of Daeya in 642, but in 660 the general Gyebaek fell with his last army at Hwangsanbeol against Kim Yu-sin, and the capital was lost.</blockquote><a href="/empire/baekje">Baekje · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sabi, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.28,126.91&amp;year=650" aria-label="Sabi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Uija of Baekje"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5475 4449h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5475 4449h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.28,126.91&amp;year=650">What was here in 650 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
